<article xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="review-article" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Pharmacol Rep</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Pharmacol Rep</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Pharmacological Reports</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">1734-1140</issn><issn pub-type="epub">2299-5684</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Springer International Publishing</publisher-name><publisher-loc>Cham</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">41134466</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12647357</article-id>
<article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">796</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/s43440-025-00796-5</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Special Issue: Review</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>The role of microbiota modulation in preventing Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease- a review</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid">http://orcid.org/0000-0002-3841-2575</contrib-id><name><surname>Strz&#x00119;pa</surname><given-names>Anna</given-names></name><address><email>a.strzepa@uj.edu.pl</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid">http://orcid.org/0000-0002-0388-2382</contrib-id><name><surname>Szczepanik</surname><given-names>Marian</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><aff id="Aff1"><institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/03bqmcz70</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="GRID">grid.5522.0</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="ISNI">0000 0001 2337 4740</institution-id><institution>Faculty of Health Sciences, Institute of Physiotherapy, Chair of Biomedical Sciences, </institution><institution>Jagiellonian University Medical College, </institution></institution-wrap>Kopernika 7a, Krak&#x000f3;w, 31-034 Poland </aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>24</day><month>10</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>24</day><month>10</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>77</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>1468</fpage><lpage>1490</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>27</day><month>6</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>9</day><month>10</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>10</day><month>10</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2025</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p><bold>Open Access</bold> This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link>.</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="Abs1" abstract-type="unstructured"><p id="Par1">Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (AD) is the most common form of dementia, and its incidence is projected to triple worldwide over the next 25 years. The most prevalent form, late-onset Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (LOAD), develops in genetically predisposed individuals exposed to environmental risk factors. Hallmarks of AD include accumulation of amyloid-&#x003b2; (A&#x003b2;), neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs), neuroinflammation, and mitochondrial dysfunction, resulting in oxidative stress, impaired glucose metabolism, and cognitive decline. Such metabolic disruptions result in early cerebral glucose hypometabolism and other metabolic disruptions, including altered lipid and branched-chain amino acid profiles. Recent evidence suggests that gut microbiota alterations, although individually variable, are a consistent and influential factor in AD progression <italic>via</italic> inflammatory and metabolic pathways. This narrative review explores therapeutic interventions targeting the gut-brain axis, including fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), probiotic and antibiotic treatments, and dietary strategies such as the ketogenic and Mediterranean diets, as well as nutritional compounds such as omega-3 fatty acids. The aim is to evaluate the latest findings in both preclinical and clinical studies to identify multi-targeted, microbiota-based approaches for the prevention and management of AD.</p></abstract><trans-abstract xml:lang="de" id="Abs2" abstract-type="unstructured"><title>Graphical Abstract</title><p id="Par2">
<graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="43440_2025_796_Figa_HTML" id="d33e172"/>
</p></trans-abstract><kwd-group xml:lang="de"><title>Keywords</title><kwd>Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease</kwd><kwd>Dementia</kwd><kwd>Diet</kwd><kwd>Gut microbiota</kwd><kwd>Prevention</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group><funding-source><institution>Polish Ministry of Science and Higher Education</institution></funding-source><award-id>N43/DBS/000345</award-id><principal-award-recipient><name><surname>Strz&#x00119;pa</surname><given-names>Anna</given-names></name></principal-award-recipient></award-group></funding-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>issue-copyright-statement</meta-name><meta-value>&#x000a9; Maj Institute of Pharmacology Polish Academy of Sciences 2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><sec id="Sec1"><title>Introduction</title><p id="Par75">Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (AD) is the most common form of dementia, with late-onset Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (LOAD) representing the predominant subtype [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref>]. LOAD typically affects individuals over the age of 65 as a result of genetic predisposition combined with exposure to environmental risk factors [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref>]. AD affects primarily the hippocampus and cerebral cortex, leading to progressive atrophy of these brain regions. Clinically, this manifests as a gradual decline in memory and cognitive functions [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR3">3</xref>]. In the early stages, patients experience difficulties with retaining newly acquired information, while in advanced stages, even deeply integrated memories, such as those involving close family members like spouses and children, may be lost [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR4">4</xref>].</p><p id="Par76">Beyond memory impairment, the degeneration of neuronal connections impairs the ability to integrate past experiences and knowledge with the current situation. This disconnection often leads to inappropriate behaviors, which cannot be corrected due to deficits in short-term memory. Notably, because motor functions decline at a much slower pace, individuals with AD require extended caregiving, placing a significant socio-economic burden on families and the healthcare system [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR4">4</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR5">5</xref>].</p><p id="Par77">As life expectancy continues to rise in many developed countries, the global incidence of AD is projected to triple over the next 25 years [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR6">6</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR7">7</xref>]. Simultaneously, declining birth rates pose a significant challenge, potentially limiting the availability of caregivers [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref>]. These demographic shifts highlight an urgent need to develop effective strategies for both prevention and treatment of AD.</p><p id="Par78">Despite numerous ongoing clinical trials, there is no approved medication that effectively halts the progressive decline in memory and cognition [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR9">9</xref>]. The complex and multifactorial nature of the disease, driven by the unique combination of genetic and environmental factors, suggests that the pathophysiological mechanisms vary between individuals [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR10">10</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR12">12</xref>]. Consequently, the approaches that target several pathways simultaneously attract growing interest.</p><p id="Par79">An emerging area of focus is the gut microbiota, which is consistently found to be altered in individuals with AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR13">13</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR14">14</xref>]. Gut microbiome is a powerful modulator of inflammation and metabolism, two processes that play a central role in AD pathogenesis [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR17">17</xref>]. These findings suggest that interventions targeting the gastrointestinal system could offer a novel and promising route for AD prevention and therapy.</p><p id="Par80">This review aims to explore the gastrointestinal-based interventions that may influence AD-associated pathology. We discuss the most recent evidence on the impact of manipulating the gut microbiota through approaches such as fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), probiotic administration, and antibiotic treatments, in both animal models and human studies [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR20">20</xref>]. In addition, we examine nutritional interventions that not only modulate gut microbiota composition but also deliver metabolites that correct metabolic dysfunctions observed in both the brain and the periphery [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">21</xref>]. Of particular interest is cerebral glucose hypometabolism, an early feature of AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR22">22</xref>], which has sparked an interest in dietary strategies that provide an alternative energy source, such as the ketogenic diet and ketone supplementation [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR23">23</xref>]. Moreover, studies have identified an inverse correlation between certain molecules - such as omega-3 fatty acids - and the risk of developing AD, suggesting their preventive potential [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>].</p><p id="Par81">The review synthesizes the latest findings on these interventions with high potential for real-world everyday strategies for preventing or mitigating the progression of AD.</p></sec><sec id="Sec2"><title>Materials and methods</title><p id="Par82">For this narrative review, we performed a comprehensive literature search in three major scientific databases: PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science. The search strategy combined multiple keywords and phrases relevant to the role of gut microbiota in AD. The primary search terms included: <italic>Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease</italic>,<italic> amyloid</italic>,<italic> antibiotics</italic>,<italic> astrogliosis</italic>,<italic> dysbiosis</italic>,<italic> environmental factors</italic>,<italic> epidemiology</italic>,<italic> fecal microbiota transplantation</italic>,<italic> genetic predispositions</italic>,<italic> gut microbiota</italic>,<italic> ketogenic diet</italic>,<italic> Mediterranean diet</italic>,<italic> metabolic changes</italic>,<italic> microgliosis</italic>,<italic> mitochondrial dysfunction</italic>,<italic> neuroinflammation</italic>,<italic> oxidative stress</italic>,<italic> pathomechanism</italic>,<italic> polyphenols</italic>,<italic> and probiotics</italic>. Preclinical (in vitro and in vivo) studies and clinical investigations were considered. For clinical studies, the impact of interventions such as antibiotics, fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), and probiotics on cognitive performance in Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease patients was evaluated through a comprehensive search of publications from all registered clinical trials conducted from 2000 onwards, with relevant earlier studies also included. Preclinical studies were analyzed to identify potential therapeutic directions and physiological and cognitive outcomes. Only articles published in peer-reviewed journals and written in English were included.</p><sec id="Sec3"><title>Epidemiology</title><p id="Par83">AD is the most common form of dementia, affecting 0.68% of the global population, with its burden increasing by over 160% since 1990, reaching 51.6 million cases and projected to rise to 152.8 million by 2050 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR6">6</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR24">24</xref>]. Incidence is highest in North Africa, the Middle East, high-income Asia Pacific, and Central Europe [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR24">24</xref>], with consistently higher rates in women than in men [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR7">7</xref>].</p><p id="Par84">The risk factors include smoking, high glucose levels, elevated BMI, and environmental exposures such as pesticides, alcohol, air pollution, and heavy metals such as aluminum, iron, mercury, zinc, and copper [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR6">6</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR10">10</xref>]. AD presents in two forms. Early-onset AD (EOAD), representing approximately 10% of cases [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>], typically develops before the age of 60, and shows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern, caused by mutation in the amyloid &#x003b2; precursor protein (APP), presenilin-1 (<italic>PSEN</italic>1), and presenilin-2 <italic>(PSEN2</italic>) genes [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR25">25</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR27">27</xref>]. LOAD, which occurs after age 65 and results from both genetic and environmental factors [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref>]. The apolipoprotein E (apoE) &#x003b5;4 allele is the strongest genetic risk factor for LOAD, significantly increasing disease risk and advancing age of onset [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR28">28</xref>]. Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have identified additional risk loci related to immune functions (complement receptor 1 (CR1), cluster of differentiation 33 (CD33), membrane-spanning 4-domain A (MS4A), clusterin (CLU), A&#x003b2; clearance, ATP-binding cassette A7 (ABCA7), ephrin receptor A1 (EPHA1), and Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA)-DRB5-DRB1, lipid metabolism (phospholipase D3 (PLD3), CLU, and ABCA7), and endocytosis and cytoskeletal dynamics (bridging integrator 1 (BIN1), phosphatidylinositol binding clathrin assembly protein (PICALM), CD2-associated protein (CD2AP), EPHA1, and sortilin-related receptor L (SORL1) are associated with endocytosis, synaptic and cytoskeletal functioning (CELF1, NME8, and CASS4) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR11">11</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR12">12</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="Sec4"><title>Pathomechanism</title><p id="Par85">AD develops through complex, interconnected processes leading to hippocampal and cortical atrophy, resulting in memory and cognitive decline. Macroscopic analysis reveals amyloid deposits and protein fragments, considered key factors in AD according to the &#x0201c;amyloid cascade hypothesis&#x0201d; (Fig. <xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR3">3</xref>].</p><p id="Par86">
<fig id="Fig1"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Neuropathological and metabolic alterations in Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease. Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (AD) is characterized by progressive neurodegeneration, marked by the accumulation of extracellular amyloid-beta (A&#x003b2;) plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs) composed of hyperphosphorylated tau. These lesions are accompanied by astrogliosis and microgliosis, which drive chronic neuroinflammation. Activated glial cells release pro-inflammatory mediators that, together with mitochondrial dysfunction, contribute to oxidative stress characterized by increased production of reactive oxygen species (ROS). Impaired mitochondrial function and structural abnormalities reduce neuronal energy production, leading to glucose hypometabolism, considered one of the earliest pathological events in AD. Collectively, these interconnected processes promote synaptic loss, neuronal death, and cognitive decline. AD: Alzheimer&#x02019;s Disease; A&#x003b2;: amyloid beta; FMT: fecal microbiota transplantation; NFTs: neurofibrillary tangles; ROS: reactive oxygen species</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="43440_2025_796_Fig1_HTML" id="d33e784"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par87">The formation of amyloid aggregates arises from a shift in the processing of APP, a transmembrane protein believed to play a role in neuronal homeostasis [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR29">29</xref>]. Normally, most APPs are processed by &#x003b1;-secretase and &#x003b3;-secretase, generating a non-amyloidogenic peptide. However, approximately 10% of APP is processed by &#x003b2;-secretase and &#x003b3;-secretase, leading to the formation of amyloidogenic peptides, primarily amyloid beta (A&#x003b2;) A&#x003b2;40 and A&#x003b2;42 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR29">29</xref>]. These peptides are prone to aggregation, forming fibrils that assemble into amyloid plaques [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR30">30</xref>]. This aggregation is promoted by conformational change in A&#x003b2;, which induces structural changes in other A&#x003b2; peptides [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">31</xref>]. The formation of A&#x003b2; plaque is closely associated with the formation of intracellular tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau protein [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR32">32</xref>], which under normal conditions stabilizes neuronal microtubules [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR33">33</xref>]. Neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs), composed of hyperphosphorylated tau, impair neuronal function and induce apoptosis. Importantly, the number of NFTs in the cortex correlates with the severity of AD-related dementia [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR34">34</xref>].</p><p id="Par88">Both Tau and A&#x003b2; aggregates trigger chronic neuroinflammation, a hallmark of AD pathology [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR35">35</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR36">36</xref>]. The use of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) before the onset of disease is associated with reduced risk of AD, implying that neuroinflammation might be one of the causative factors [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR17">17</xref>]. Neuroinflammation is driven by the activated microglia and astrocytes, which release inflammatory cytokines and immune mediators, leading to mitochondrial dysfunction and increased production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR37">37</xref>]. The functional and morphological changes in glial cells are termed microgliosis and astrogliosis, respectively.</p><p id="Par89">Microglia are the brain&#x02019;s resident phagocytes responsible for maintaining homeostasis [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR38">38</xref>]. They play a crucial role in the clearance of A&#x003b2; [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR39">39</xref>], although this process can be altered by changes in microglia phenotype during AD progression [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR40">40</xref>]. Astrocytes, the most abundant glial cells in the central nervous system (CNS) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref>], regulate cerebral blood flow in response to metabolic demands - a mechanism known as neurovascular coupling. During AD progression, astrocytes undergo transcriptional changes marked by increased expression of genes related to inflammation and lipid metabolism [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR42">42</xref>].</p><p id="Par90">Several studies also highlight increased oxidative stress in AD. In the neurons of AD patients, the elevated level of 8-oxoguanine - a marker of oxidative DNA damage - has been observed [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR43">43</xref>], alongside reduced levels of antioxidant glutathione (GSH) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR44">44</xref>]. Oxidative stress contributes to the formation of A&#x003b2; plaques and NFTs [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR45">45</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR46">46</xref>]. ROS are produced not only by activated microglia, astrocytes, and immune cells infiltrating the CNS, but also originate primarily from mitochondria, which are both a source and a target of oxidative stress [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR47">47</xref>]. In AD neurons, mitochondria exhibit altered morphology and number, changes thought to be influenced by deposition of oligomeric A&#x003b2; [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR48">48</xref>].</p><p id="Par91">Since the mitochondria are the primary source of cellular ATP, their dysfunction compromises the brain&#x02019;s energy demands. Positron emission tomography (PET) using 18 F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) has shown reduced glucose uptake in the cortex, a decline that worsens with the progression of AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR22">22</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR49">49</xref>]. This hypometabolism may result from reduced expression of glucose transporters (GLUTs) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR50">50</xref>] and insulin resistance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR51">51</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR52">52</xref>]. Additionally, key enzymes involved in glycolysis, the tricarboxylic acids (TCA), and the electron transport chain (ETC) show reduced activity and levels in AD neurons [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR53">53</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR54">54</xref>]. Notably, glucose hypometabolism precedes cognitive decline in genetically predisposed individuals, suggesting it may be one of the earliest pathological events in AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR55">55</xref>]. In support of this, intranasal insulin treatment has been shown to improve memory in AD patients [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR56">56</xref>].</p><p id="Par92">Metabolic alterations in AD also affect lipids and amino acids, particularly branched-chain amino acids [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR57">57</xref>]. Metabolomics studies have found decreased total phospholipid levels and increased levels of their breakdown products - glycerophosphocholine and glycerophosphoethanolamine &#x02013; in the frontal cortex and hippocampus of AD patients [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR58">58</xref>]. Additionally, increased levels of ceramide [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR59">59</xref>] and cholesterol [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR60">60</xref>] have been reported. Lipids in the AD brain also undergo qualitative changes, such as oxidation of double bonds [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR61">61</xref>]. Interestingly, the higher intake of polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA), including &#x003b1;-linolenic acid, eicosatetraenoic acid (EPA), and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), which together can make up to 35% of brain lipids, has been associated with protection against AD development [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR62">62</xref>], with their increased brain levels correlating negatively with AD pathology [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>].</p><p id="Par93">The accumulation of A&#x003b2;, glial cell dysfunctions, mitochondrial impairment, and disrupted energy metabolism underscores that AD arises from complex, interrelated mechanisms. These intricate pathomechanisms suggest that early alteration leading to LOAD may vary between individuals, highlighting the potential benefit of personalized early interventions.</p></sec><sec id="Sec5"><title>Gut microbiota modulates AD</title><p id="Par94">The human body, especially the gastrointestinal tract, is inhabited by bacteria, archaea, fungi, and viruses, collectively known as microbiota. The gut microbiota provides a biological barrier against external microbial threats and is regarded as an &#x0201c;additional&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;virtual&#x0201d; organ of the body due to its role in metabolism, immunoregulation, and nutrient absorption [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref>]. The gut microbiota is an extremely complex yet dynamic entity, functionally connected with the liver, lungs, and brain. Accordingly, alterations in gut microbiota are associated with immune-mediated, metabolic, and neurodegenerative diseases, including AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR63">63</xref>].</p><p id="Par95">Several human studies have shown that alterations in gut microbiota composition are associated with the development of AD. Patients with very mild or mild dementia show reduced microbial richness and abundance compared to healthy controls. Compositional differences at the phylum level include a decrease in <italic>Firmicutes</italic> and <italic>Actinobacteria</italic>, and an increase in <italic>Bacteroidetes</italic>, particularly the family <italic>Bifidobacteriaceae</italic> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR13">13</xref>]. Another study in patients with mild dementia confirmed structural alteration of the gut microbiota at family, genus, species, and operational taxonomic unit (OTU) levels. However, the changes at the phylum level were somewhat opposite, that is, a decrease in <italic>Bacteroidetes</italic>, an increase <italic>in Actinobacteria</italic>,<italic> and</italic> no change in <italic>Firmicutes</italic> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR14">14</xref>]. Yet another analysis in patients with mild cognitive impairment (MCI) reported no changes in alpha diversity (richness) as well as similarity and dissimilarity between groups, but a decrease in abundance of the genera <italic>Ruminococcus</italic>,<italic> Butyricimonas</italic>, and <italic>Oxalobacter</italic>, and an increase in abundance in <italic>Flavonifractor</italic> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR64">64</xref>].</p><p id="Par96">Linking these alterations to AD pathology, some studies have examined whether changes in gut microbiota composition could be associated with specific features of AD, such as A&#x003b2; or tau aggregates. In a study limited to a few candidate species, A&#x003b2;-positive patients showed a lower abundance of the anti-inflammatory <italic>Eubacterium rectale</italic> and a higher abundance of proinflammatory <italic>Escherichia/Shigella</italic> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR65">65</xref>]. More extensive analysis confirmed that A&#x003b2;-positive patients had a reduced abundance of <italic>Eubacterium</italic>, specifically the <italic>Eubacterium ventriosum</italic> group, as well as decreased abundance of <italic>Lachnospiraceae</italic> spp. and <italic>Roseburia hominis</italic>, among others, along with an increased abundance of <italic>Clostridium leptum.</italic> In phosphorylated-tau positive patients, a lower abundance of <italic>Lachnospiraceae</italic> spp., <italic>Lachnoclostridium</italic> spp., <italic>Roseburia hominis</italic>, and <italic>Bilophila wadsworthia</italic> was observed [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR66">66</xref>]. These findings suggest that there is no single, consistent microbial signature associated with A&#x003b2; and tau positivity, but rather a pattern of overlapping and variable alterations in gut microbiota composition.</p><p id="Par97">Beyond compositional changes, the gut microbiota is the source of the molecules and microbial products detectable in the bloodstream and in the brain, particularly after crossing a disrupted blood-brain barrier (BBB). Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) and <italic>Escherichia coli</italic>-derived K99 proteins are found in the brains of healthy individuals and AD patients. However, their levels are significantly higher post-mortem brain tissue of AD patients and colocalize with A&#x003b2; plaque [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR67">67</xref>]. Additionally, brain tissues obtained from AD patients show a 5- to 10-fold increase in the abundance of bacteria, specifically <italic>Firmicutes</italic> &#x0201c;other&#x0201d;, <italic>Staphylococcus</italic>, and <italic>Propionibacteriaceae acnes</italic> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR68">68</xref>]. However, it appears that bacteria present in the AD brain may originate from the oral microbiota, as poor oral health - such as infrequent teeth brushing and tooth loss - has been associated with an increased risk of developing dementia in elderly adults [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR69">69</xref>]. One of the oral microbes strongly linked to AD pathology is <italic>Porphyromonas gingivalis.</italic> This bacterium has been detected in brain tissue obtained from AD patients with A&#x003b2; aggregates, and oral infection in mice led to brain colonization and increased A&#x003b2; production [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR70">70</xref>].</p><p id="Par98">Complementary evidence comes from animal models. Eight- to twelve-month-old AD mice exhibited a distinct community structure, characterized by a prominent increase <italic>in Verrucomicrobia</italic> and <italic>Proteobacteria</italic>, and a significant decrease in <italic>Ruminococcus</italic> and <italic>Butyricicoccus</italic> compared to control mice [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR71">71</xref>]. Gut microbiota analysis in 5xFAD mice (Five Familial AD Mutations) showed an increased Firmicutes/Bacteroidetes ratio at nine weeks of age [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR72">72</xref>], while analysis of germ-free (GF) 3&#x000d7;Tg mice colonized with fecal samples from AD patients and controls showed the opposite trend [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR73">73</xref>]. Although these studies do not reveal a uniform pattern of gut microbiota alteration, they consistently demonstrate that gut microbiota structure is altered during the development of AD.</p><p id="Par99">In addition to compositional changes, functional alterations in microbial metabolites have been reported [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR74">74</xref>]. Brain tissue of AD patients is characterized by major alterations in lipid and amino acid metabolism [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>]. In line with this, metabolomic studies of fecal samples show changes in short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) and tryptophan levels during AD development in humans [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>]. Gut microbiota produces SCFAs, and their levels - specifically acetic acid, butyric acid, and caproic acid- were found to be significantly lower in MCI patients compared to normal controls [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR76">76</xref>], and negatively correlated with A&#x003b2; deposition in brain regions associated with cognition [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR76">76</xref>]. Similar alterations, characterized by decreased levels of butyric acid and iso-butyric acid in both feces and brain tissue, were observed in animal models of AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR71">71</xref>].</p><p id="Par100">Dietary choline and carnitine can be converted by intestinal microbes into trimethylamine (TMA), which is subsequently oxidized to trimethylamine N-oxide (TMAO). TMAO has been shown to exacerbate age-related cognitive dysfunction in mice [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR77">77</xref>]. However, no differences in TMAO levels in the CNS have been observed between AD patients and cognitively normal controls [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR78">78</xref>], and its plasma levels do not affect cognition or increase the risk of developing dementia in humans [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR79">79</xref>]. In contrast, higher plasma levels of its precursor choline have been associated with poorer cognition, brain atrophy, and vascular dysfunction, whereas increased plasma levels of carnitine, another TMAO precursor, have been linked to reduced vascular dysfunction in white matter in humans [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR79">79</xref>]. Together, these findings illustrate the complexity of metabolic interactions between TMAO and its precursors, and highlight the importance of choline and carnitine metabolism for cognitive performance.</p><p id="Par101">Gut microbiota is also responsible for the generation of tryptophan metabolites, such as indoles, kynurenine, serotonin, and tryptamine [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR80">80</xref>]. AD patients show significantly lower urine and/or serum levels of tryptophan and tryptophan metabolites, such as serotonin, 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid, kynurenine, kynurenic acid, and xanthurenic acid [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR81">81</xref>]. These changes were more pronounced in AD patients than in the MCI group [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR81">81</xref>]. Additionally, the formation of indole derivatives, which act as aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AhR) ligands, was significantly reduced [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>]. A meta-analysis involving 22 clinical studies confirmed the association between AD and the alteration of tryptophan metabolism, showing a general decrease in tryptophan and kynurenic acid levels in the blood, alongside an increase in kynurenic acid in the cerebrospinal fluid and an elevated kynurenine-to-tryptophan ratio in blood [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR82">82</xref>]. This suggests that tryptophan metabolism is shifted towards the kynurenine pathway [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR82">82</xref>]. Alterations in tryptophan metabolites in AD patients are linked to changes in the gut microbiota. A positive correlation was observed between <italic>Clostridia</italic> and DL-5-methoxytryptophan (DL-5-MTP), as well as between <italic>Ruminococcus</italic> order and indole-2-carboxylic acid, whereas a negative correlation was observed with indole-3-pyruvic acid (IPYA) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>]. Interestingly, increased levels of IPYA were negatively correlated, while levels of indole-2-carboxylic acid were positively correlated with cognitive impairment in AD patients, supporting the link between dysbiosis-induced alteration in tryptophan metabolism and AD-associated cognitive decline [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>].</p><p id="Par102">Alterations in gut microbiota composition and metabolites are consistently reported in AD, including reduced SCFAs and dysregulated tryptophan metabolism. While no uniform microbial signature has been identified, these findings indicate that strategies targeting microbiota modulation may hold adjunctive or therapeutic potential in AD management.</p></sec><sec id="Sec6"><title>Microbiota modulations in AD</title><p id="Par103">The gut microbiota influences the development of AD by affecting multiple mechanisms involved in its progression [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR13">13</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR14">14</xref>], and alterations in gut microbes are associated with positive health outcomes [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR20">20</xref>]. Since there is no effective pharmacological therapy for AD, strategies that disrupt AD pathomechanisms by influencing the gut microbiota are extremely valuable [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR9">9</xref>]. Gut microbes could be modulated directly through FMT, antibiotic or probiotic use, or indirectly, by applying molecules or dietary approaches that target the structure or metabolic functions of the gut microbiota.</p><sec id="Sec7"><title>FMT</title><p id="Par104">FMT is a procedure in which fecal bacterial content, consisting of the whole microbial community and its metabolites, is transferred from a healthy donor to a patient. The procedure was first used in 1958 to treat recurrent <italic>Clostridium difficile</italic> infection (CDI), and it has been used ever since due to its effectiveness [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR83">83</xref>]. FMT has also shown effectiveness in the amelioration of inflammatory bowel disease, metabolic syndrome, and autoimmune disorders [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR84">84</xref>]. Several studies using different mouse models of AD have revealed that FMT can ameliorate AD-associated cognitive decline (Table <xref rid="Tab1" ref-type="table">1</xref>).</p><p id="Par105">Early stages of AD development are studied using transgenic APPswe/PS1dE9 (APP/PS1) mice, which carry human <italic>APP</italic> and <italic>PSEN1</italic> genes with mutations and exhibit delayed A&#x003b2; plaque formation without tau pathology. FMT treatment has been shown to improve cognitive decline, which is associated with reduced A&#x003b2; burden and phosphorylation of tau protein, as well as reduced neuroinflammation due to lower levels of myeloid cells in the cortex and the hippocampal regions [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR85">85</xref>]. The FMT procedure also reduced expression of the key proinflammatory enzyme cyclooxygenase (COX), while more detailed studies showed that FMT limits inflammation by regulating the toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4)/MyD88/NF-&#x003ba;B signaling pathway, which was associated with the restoration of gut microbiota balance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR86">86</xref>].</p><p id="Par106">A familiar type of AD with early and aggressive presentation due to rapid A&#x003b2; deposition is recapitulated by 5xFAD mice, which harbor human <italic>APP</italic> and <italic>PSEN1</italic> transgenes with five familiar AD mutations. FMT administered once a week in 5xFAD mice was not effective in ameliorating AD development [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR87">87</xref>], although another study showed that FMT from young 8&#x02013;10-week-old wild-type donors administered to 5xFAD mice, which are a model of AD, resulted in significant improvements in cognitive scores and decreased A&#x003b2; burden [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref>]. However, FMT performed every other day for eight weeks prevented cognitive decline, although its effects diminished after treatment termination [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR87">87</xref>]. The beneficial effects of FMT were related to reduced deposition of A&#x003b2;, due to its decreased expression and processing by BACE1 (&#x003b2;-site APP cleaving enzyme 1), as well as ameliorated neuroinflammation resulting from reduced LPS-induced signaling [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR87">87</xref>]. Attenuated LPS-induced TLR4 signaling was associated with lower levels of LPS in the brain, which may be a consequence of decreased intestinal permeability following FMT [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR87">87</xref>].</p><p id="Par107">The DLP<sup>APT</sup> mouse model, which carries human <italic>APP</italic>, <italic>PESN1</italic>, and <italic>Tau</italic> genes with predisposing mutations under neuron-specific promoter, recapitulates key characteristics of AD, including A&#x003b2; and tau tangles deposition. Daily FMT for 16 weeks using normal fecal microbiota ameliorated cognitive decline, which was associated with reduced AD-related pathology, namely A&#x003b2; and tau-tangles deposition, and decreased activation of glial cells [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR88">88</xref>]. The protective effects of FMT may result from restoration of intestinal integrity, likely due to reversal of macrophage-associated immune dysfunctions [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR88">88</xref>].</p><p id="Par108">Similarly, AD-prone Tau/APOE4 (TE4) mice, which carry a mutated <italic>Tau</italic> transgene and <italic>APOE4</italic> gene, raised in the GF environment, are protected from AD-associated neurodegeneration, as measured by neuron loss and changes in hippocampal and lateral ventricular volume. However, colonization with a microbial community obtained from conventionally raised E4 mice abolished this protection [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR89">89</xref>], clearly implying that gut microbiota alterations may be among the causative factors associated with AD. Accordingly, FMT using healthy microbiota could offer a protective strategy.</p><p id="Par109">In line with FMT studies using mouse models, clinical data also confirm the beneficial role of restoring a healthy microbial community through the FMT procedure (Table <xref rid="Tab2" ref-type="table">2</xref>). The literature reports the case of an 82-year-old man with MCI due to AD, who was suffering from recurrent CDI. The man received a single transplantation of fecal microbiota obtained from his wife. The procedure effectively eliminated <italic>C. difficile</italic> infection and resulted in improved AD symptoms. Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) two months after FMT showed improved scores, which placed him within the cognitively normal range. The scores improved further over the following four months [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR90">90</xref>]. Similarly, treatment of a 90-year-old woman with CDI via FMT resulted in improved cognitive function compared to her performance prior to the procedure. This improvement was associated with modifications in the gut microbiota composition, characterized by the enrichment of <italic>Bacteroidales</italic>, <italic>Bacteroidia</italic>, <italic>Tannerellaceae</italic>, and <italic>Actinobacteria &#x02013;</italic> taxa previously implicated in cognitive functioning [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR91">91</xref>].</p><p id="Par110">A small clinical study showed a moderate impact of FMT on cognition, but only in MCI patients. FMT was administered in the form of 40 specially prepared pills, each containing 1 g of healthy fecal material, and given three times at two-week intervals [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR92">92</xref>]. Gut microbiota analysis revealed structural changes, characterized by an increase in <italic>Prevotella</italic> and a decrease in <italic>Lachnospira</italic>. Serum metabolomic analysis indicated changes with lipids, associated with bile secretion and metabolism of choline, linoleic acid, linolenic acid, and glycerophospholipids [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR92">92</xref>].</p><p id="Par111">In addition to the alterations in gut microbiota composition and metabolomic profiles, the FMT procedure was only slightly effective for patients with MCI compared to those with more severe impairment. Only the former group demonstrated improved cognitive scores as measured by Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA), but not by AD Assessment Scale &#x02013; Cognitive Subscale (ADAS-Cog), which is widely used in AD clinical trials but offers less sensitivity than MoCA [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR92">92</xref>]. No impact of FMT on cognitive performance was observed in a patient with severe cognitive impairment. However, no further cognitive decline was observed in either group following FMT. The validity of these findings is limited by the fact that the trial was designed as a single-arm study without a control group [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR92">92</xref>].</p><p id="Par112">Nevertheless, the results are consistent with another study involving five patients with AD and recurrent CDI [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR93">93</xref>]. Three months after FMT, gut microbiota composition had shifted, gene expression involved in lipid metabolism was modulated, and cognitive functions significantly improved as measured by MoCA and MMSE. Although this study was also single-arm, it demonstrated a similar mechanism of action of FMT as the previous trial, implying that further studies on FMT could be promising [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR93">93</xref>].</p><p id="Par113">Additionally, it has to be mentioned that the FMT procedure, though relatively safe, may cause adverse effects (AE) in 28.5% of patients [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR94">94</xref>]. The most common AE was abdominal discomfort, especially after FMT via the upper gastrointestinal route, followed by fever in 3.4% of patients. Other minor AEs, such as constipation, altered stool frequency, vomiting, and cramping, were seen in fewer patients and generally do not affect clinical outcomes. About 9.2% of patients developed serious AEs, such as autoimmune disease, FMT-related injury, or severe infection [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR94">94</xref>].</p><p id="Par114">The presented data show that the treatment regimen varies across animal and human studies. In the two reported clinical cases, a single FMT effectively improved cognition, which contrasts with findings from animal studies where multiple FMTs are required to modify the severity of AD-associated symptoms [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR85">85</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR88">88</xref>]. This discrepancy may be explained by the fact that AD development in mice is driven by modifications of genes directly involved in AD pathology [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR87">87</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR88">88</xref>]. As a result, these disease models more closely resemble EOAD, the familial form of AD caused by genetic alterations and characterized by early onset [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>]. By contrast, the two patients successfully treated with FMT were, based on their age, most likely suffering from LOAD, which arises from the interplay of multiple pathological alterations [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref>]. In such patients, FMT may act on several pathological mechanisms simultaneously, thereby contributing to cognitive improvements - a strategy that may be less effective when the genetic factors dominate disease progression, as is observed in EOAD. It is noteworthy that part of the observed cognitive improvement could be attributable to general health benefits following FMT, particularly the alleviation of diarrhea-related water loss.</p><p id="Par115">However, the strength of evidence varies considerably across these reports. Case reports, such as those describing individual patients with MCI or AD, provide valuable insights and generate hypotheses, but their clinical significance is limited by their anecdotal nature and lack of controls [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR90">90</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR91">91</xref>]. Small uncontrolled studies add further suggestive evidence, but are still prone to bias [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR92">92</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR93">93</xref>]. In contrast, randomized controlled trials (RCTs), which remain the gold standard for assessing therapeutic efficacy, are required to draw reliable conclusions regarding the clinical utility of FMT in AD. The current findings, while encouraging, should therefore be interpreted with caution until validated by larger, well-designed RCTs.</p><p id="Par116">
<table-wrap id="Tab1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Effects of fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) on AD pathology and cognitive function in Transgenic mouse models</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Research model</th><th align="left">Effects</th><th align="left">References</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">APP/PS1 transgenic mice</td><td align="left">Improved cognitive function; reduced A&#x003b2; and tau phosphorylation; reduced neuroinflammation</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR85">85</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR86">86</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">5xFAD transgenic mice</td><td align="left">Prevented cognitive decline; reduced A&#x003b2; deposition; decreased inflammation; improved intestinal barrier.</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR87">87</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">DLP<sup>APT</sup> transgenic mice</td><td align="left">Improved cognition; reduced A&#x003b2; and tau deposition; decreased neuroinflammation; restored intestinal integrity</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR88">88</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Tau/APOE4 (TE4) mice in germ-free (GF) conditions</td><td align="left">GF mice protected from neurodegeneration; colonization with microbiota from conventionally raised TE4 mice abolished protection</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR89">89</xref>]</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Abbreviations: 5xFAD: Five Familial Alzheimer&#x02019;s Disease Mutation (Transgenic Mouse Model of AD); APP/PS1: amyloid precursor protein/presenilin 1 double transgenic mouse model of AD; A&#x003b2;: amyloid beta; AD: Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease; FMT: fecal microbiota transplantation; DLP<sup>APT</sup>: transgenic mouse model of AD carrying human mutations in <italic>APP</italic>, <italic>PSEN1</italic>, and <italic>MAPT/Tau</italic> genes; GF: germ-free; TE4: Tau/APOE4 transgenic mice</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></p><p id="Par117">
<table-wrap id="Tab2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Effects of fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) on cognitive function and gut microbiota in clinical studies of Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (AD)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Type of the study</th><th align="left">Effects</th><th align="left">References</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Small clinical trial, patients with MCI or advanced AD</td><td align="left">Cognitive improvement in the MCI group; cognitive decline halted in both groups; gut microbiota shift; altered serum lipid</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR92">92</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">5 patients with AD with rCDI</td><td align="left">Cognitive improvement, gut microbiota shift, altered serum lipid metabolism</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR93">93</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Clinical case: 82-year-old man with AD and CDI</td><td align="left">Cognitive improvement after FMT administered for CDI</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR90">90</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Clinical case: 90-year-old woman with AD and CDI</td><td align="left">Improved cognitive function; microbiota shift towards those associated with cognition</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR91">91</xref>]</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Abbreviations: AD: Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease; CDI: <italic>Clostridium difficile</italic> infection; FMT: fecal microbiota transplantation; GF: germ-free; MCI: mild cognitive impairment; rCDI: recurrent <italic>Clostridium difficile</italic> infection</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></p></sec><sec id="Sec8"><title>Antibiotics</title><p id="Par118">Antibiotics are among the most commonly used medications, intended to eliminate pathogenic bacteria from the human body [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR63">63</xref>]. However, their application also reduces the number of commensal bacteria as an unintended consequence, leading to alterations in the structure of microbial communities, which are specific to the type of antibiotic used [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR63">63</xref>].</p><p id="Par119">AD is associated with alterations in gut microbiota composition [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR88">88</xref>]. Additionally, brain tissue obtained from AD patients contains nearly ten times more bacteria than brain tissue from healthy counterparts [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR68">68</xref>]. A&#x003b2; and its tendency to aggregate have been implicated as a protective mechanism that limits microbial spread throughout the brain, suggesting that the formation of A&#x003b2;, a key mediator of AD, may arise as a part of an immune response to bacterial presence in the CNS [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR95">95</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR96">96</xref>]. Supporting this concept, brain infection of AD-prone 5xFAD mice with <italic>Salmonella Typhimurium</italic> leads to the formation of A&#x003b2; aggregates that colocalize with bacteria, resembling microbial entrapment [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR95">95</xref>]. Additionally, soluble A&#x003b2; oligomers bind to the surface of pathogens, limiting their adhesion to host cells, and have been shown to be toxic to microorganisms [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR96">96</xref>]. The anti-bacterial mechanism of A&#x003b2; formation may be further enhanced by certain bacteria, through bacterially expressed amyloids such as curli [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR97">97</xref>], which, due to their structure, exhibit cross-seeding activity toward A&#x003b2;. Furthermore, the process of A&#x003b2; formation appears to be regulated by the gut microbiota [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR98">98</xref>].</p><p id="Par120">The protective effect of microbiota absence has been demonstrated in germ-free (GF) models. The APP transgenic mice and TE4 mice, which spontaneously develop protein aggregates in the brain, exhibit lower levels of A&#x003b2; aggregates or phosphorylated tau when raised in GF conditions, compared to conventionally raised animals [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR89">89</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR99">99</xref>]. Colonization of GF TE4 mice with microbiota obtained from conventionally raised TE4 mice reversed this protective effect, as evidenced by increased neuronal loss and decreased volumes of the hippocampus and lateral ventricles [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR89">89</xref>].</p><p id="Par121">Animal studies further support the role of antibiotics in modulating microbiota and AD pathology. The alteration of gut microbiota composition, as well as the amyloid-seeding potential of microbiota infecting the brain, suggests that the antibiotics that remove certain bacterial species could potentially be beneficial in the amelioration of AD, which has been explored using animal models of the disease (Table <xref rid="Tab3" ref-type="table">3</xref>). Early-life treatment of APP<sub>SWE</sub>/PS1<sub>&#x00394;E9</sub> AD-prone mice with an antibiotic mixture consisting of gentamicin, vancomycin, metronidazole, neomycin, ampicillin, kanamycin, colistin, and cefoperazone, administered from day 14 to 21, resulted in decreased levels of A&#x003b2; six months later; however, the study did not assess whether these changes in A&#x003b2; deposition affected cognitive function [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR100">100</xref>]. A similar reduction in brain A&#x003b2; was observed when the antibiotic mixture was administered not only early in life but also continuously for the following six months. This change was associated with an increased abundance of <italic>Lachnospiraceae</italic> and S24-7 when the antibiotic mixture was given only early in life, or with <italic>Akkermansia</italic> and <italic>Lachnospiraceae</italic> when the treatment was prolonged. These alterations in microbiota composition were linked to a reduced inflammatory response, likely due to elevated levels of Foxp3<sup>+</sup> T regulatory cells and reduced activation of astrocytes and microglia [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR100">100</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR101">101</xref>].</p><p id="Par122">The protective impact of antibiotics was also observed when an antibiotic mixture containing vancomycin, cefoxitin, gentamicin, and metronidazole was administered during the early stages of disease. 5xFAD mice received an antibiotic mixture for two months starting at two months of age, which coincides with the development of spontaneous A&#x003b2; aggregates. The treatment reduced the level of A&#x003b2; aggregates both shortly after administration and ten months later, resulting in attenuated neuronal loss and cognitive decline, implying that antibiotic treatment could serve as a therapeutic intervention even at the later stages of disease [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR102">102</xref>]. Interestingly, administration of the antibiotics mixture influenced the transcriptional profile of microglial cells, leading to downregulation of gene expression related to neuroinflammation and chemokine signaling, which are associated with AD susceptibility [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR102">102</xref>].</p><p id="Par123">Bioinformatic analysis suggested a role for interleukin (IL)-17A and tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-&#x003b1;) signaling in the microbiota-dependent regulation of AD-related pathology [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR103">103</xref>]. Indeed, a study using APP transgenic mice treated for two months with a mixture of vancomycin, ampicillin, neomycin sulfate, streptomycin, and metronidazole to deplete host microbiota showed amelioration of AD-associated pathology, which was abrogated in IL-17A knockout mice. The antibiotic-induced microbiota depletion, in an IL-17A-dependent manner, reduced TNF-&#x003b1; production and increased the expression of LDL Receptor-Related Protein (LRP1) and ATP-Binding Cassette Sub-Family B Member 1 (ABCB1) transporters at the BBB, both responsible for A&#x003b2; efflux [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR104">104</xref>].</p><p id="Par124">The aforementioned studies used mixtures of antibiotics to target a broad spectrum of microorganisms, resulting in substantial microbiota depletion. A more clinically relevant approach - relying not on the broad-spectrum depletion of commensal microbes but on the use of doxycycline alone, normalized gut microbiota diversity changes induced by the onset of plaque formation in APP transgenic mice, and prevented A&#x003b2; formation in cortex and hippocampus, although the study did not assess the impact of these changes on cognitive performance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR105">105</xref>]. Although the results obtained from mouse AD models appear relatively clear-cut, clinical data remain more ambiguous (Table <xref rid="Tab4" ref-type="table">4</xref>).</p><p id="Par125">Clinical studies, however, present a more complex picture (Table <xref rid="Tab4" ref-type="table">4</xref>). The primary reason for using antibiotics in patients with AD is the treatment of infections. Eradication of pulmonary pathogen <italic>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</italic> is achieved through treatment with D-Cycloserine, an antibiotic that is a partial agonist of N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor, whose role has been implicated in AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR106">106</xref>]. AD patients treated with D-cycloserine for four weeks showed improved cognitive function, as measured by the AD Assessment Scale [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR107">107</xref>]. This is in line with the potential of D-cycloserine to support synaptic plasticity, and thereby learning and memory [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR108">108</xref>]. However, a later study did not confirm the therapeutic potential of D-cycloserine in AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR109">109</xref>]. These inconsistent findings may be related to the variable roles of NMDA receptor subunits in AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR110">110</xref>], suggesting that further research is needed to clarify the role of NMDA signaling at different stages of disease.</p><p id="Par126">Moreover, certain infections are more prevalent in AD patients than in cognitively normal individuals, suggesting a potential causal link between infection and AD. Approximately 88% of AD patients are infected with <italic>Helicobacter pylori</italic>, compared to 47% of controls [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR111">111</xref>]. Eradication of <italic>H. pylori</italic> with a combination of two antibiotics - clarithromycin and amoxicillin - was successful in 85% of AD patients and resulted in improved cognitive scores recorded two years after the treatment, compared to those in whom eradication was unsuccessful [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR112">112</xref>].</p><p id="Par127">Similarly, infection with <italic>Chlamydia pneumoniae</italic> has been associated with increased risk of developing AD, and <italic>Chlamydia</italic> antigens have been found in the brains of AD patients [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR113">113</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR114">114</xref>]. Eradication of <italic>Chlamydia pneumoniae</italic> is achieved using tetracyclines and rifampin, agents that also inhibit amyloid aggregation [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR115">115</xref>]. In one study, AD patients with mild to moderate AD treated for 3 months with microbiota-modifying antibiotics, doxycycline and rifampin, showed less cognitive decline 6 months after the end of treatment, as measured by the Standardized AD Assessment Scale (SADAS) cognitive subscale (SADAScog) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR116">116</xref>]. Since neither group was infected with <italic>Chlamydia pneumoniae</italic>, the improved outcomes were not due to bacterial eradication. However, a larger study involving 406 patients over 12 months found that treatment with doxycycline or rifampin alone had a negative impact on cognitive performance, while the combination of both antibiotics did not affect cognition [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR117">117</xref>]. In parallel with this, a 24-month treatment with another tetracycline, minocycline, showed no effect on the cognitive performance of AD patients with mild symptoms [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR118">118</xref>]. Although these findings appear contradictory, they suggest that the therapeutic potential of antibiotics in AD may not be related to their antimicrobial activity against specific AD-associated pathogens [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR107">107</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR116">116</xref>], but rather to their broader effects on host microbiota composition [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR117">117</xref>].</p><p id="Par128">Large-scale epidemiological data add another dimension. Although the use of antibiotics alters microbiota composition, and such alterations may impact AD development, their use in healthy adults does not increase the risk of developing dementia [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR20">20</xref>]. Interestingly, a large retrospective study of more than 35,000 dementia cases over 12 years revealed that prior use of antibiotics was actually associated with a decreased risk of developing dementia. This reduced likelihood was observed for antibiotics targeting various groups of microbes, including tetracyclines, penicillins, other &#x003b2;-lactam antibacterials, macrolides, lincosamides, streptogramins, and quinolone antibacterials [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR119">119</xref>].</p><p id="Par129">In summary, preclinical studies strongly support a protective effect of antibiotics through microbiota modulation and reduction of A&#x003b2; pathology, while clinical findings remain inconsistent and limited in strength. While some randomized controlled trials, such as those testing doxycycline and rifampin combinations, demonstrated a modest slowing of cognitive decline [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR116">116</xref>], others found no measurable benefit or even detrimental effects of antibiotic monotherapy [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR117">117</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR118">118</xref>]. Small-scale, double-blind studies using agents like D-cycloserine reported short-term cognitive improvements [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR107">107</xref>], yet these findings lack replication in larger cohorts. Observational studies, including <italic>H. pylori</italic> eradication trials and large epidemiological analyses, suggest a potential link between antibiotic exposure and improved cognitive outcomes or reduced dementia risk [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR112">112</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR119">119</xref>]. However, the heterogeneity in study design and variable endpoints significantly weakens the overall strength of the evidence. Collectively, current data do not provide conclusive support for antibiotic therapy as an effective or generalizable intervention in AD. This highlights the need for further research to clarify the therapeutic potential, including the optimal dosage, timing, and type of antibiotics used. This is particularly important, given the risk of antibiotic resistance, which may lead to the outgrowth of certain microbial species.</p><p id="Par130">
<table-wrap id="Tab3"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Effects of antibiotic interventions on Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (AD) pathology and cognitive function in transgenic mouse models</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Intervention</th><th align="left">Research model</th><th align="left">Effects</th><th align="left">References</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics - mixture (gentamicin, vancomycin, etc.)</td><td align="left">APP<sub>SWE</sub>/PS1&#x00394;E9 mice (early life &#x00026; long-term)</td><td align="left">Reduced A&#x003b2; levels, increased Foxp3<sup>+</sup> Tregs, reduced neuroinflammation</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR100">100</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR101">101</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics - mixture (vancomycin, cefoxitin</td><td align="left">5xFAD mice</td><td align="left">Reduced A&#x003b2; aggregates, attenuated cognitive decline, and reduced neuroinflammation.</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR102">102</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics &#x02013; anisomycin</td><td align="left">15-month-old 3xTg-AD mice</td><td align="left">Improved memory, reduced A&#x003b2; and tau, reduced neuroinflammation</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR103">103</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics &#x02013; mixture (vancomycin, ampicillin, neomycin)</td><td align="left">APP mice (IL-17A dependent)</td><td align="left">Reduced AD-associated pathology, supports A&#x003b2; efflux at the BBB</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR104">104</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics- doxycycline monotherapy</td><td align="left">APP mice</td><td align="left">Reduced A&#x003b2;</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR105">105</xref>]</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Abbreviations: 3xTg-AD: triple transgenic mouse model of AD; AD: Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease; APP/PS1: amyloid precursor protein/presenilin 1 double transgenic mouse of AD; APP<sub>SWE</sub>: Swedish mutation transgenic mouse model of AD; A&#x003b2;: amyloid-beta; BBB: blood-brain barrier; Foxp3 + Tregs: forkhead box P3 positive regulatory T cells; IL-17A: interleukin-17A</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></p><p id="Par131">
<table-wrap id="Tab4"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Effects of antibiotic interventions on cognitive function in Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (AD) patients</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Intervention</th><th align="left">Type of the study</th><th align="left">Effects</th><th align="left">References</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics &#x02013; minocycline</td><td align="left">Multicenter, double-blind randomized clinical trial</td><td align="left">No effect on cognition</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR118">118</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics - doxycycline or rifampin alone vs. combination</td><td align="left">Multicenter, blinded, randomized clinical trial</td><td align="left">Mono-treatment worsened cognition, combo had no effect</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR117">117</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics - doxycycline + rifampin</td><td align="left">Randomized, triple-blind, controlled trial</td><td align="left">Slower cognitive decline over 12 months</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR116">116</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics - D-cycloserine</td><td align="left">Double-blind, placebo-controlled trial</td><td align="left">Improved cognitive function (ADAS score)</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR107">107</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics - clarithromycin + amoxicillin</td><td align="left">Observational study with interventional eradication regimen (<italic>H. pylori</italic>-infected AD patients)</td><td align="left">Eradication success improved cognitive scores over 2 years</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR112">112</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Antibiotics</td><td align="left">Epidemiological study in &#x0003e;35,000 dementia cases</td><td align="left">Prior antibiotic use is associated with decreased dementia risk</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR119">119</xref>]</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Abbreviations: AD: Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease; ADAS: Alzheimer&#x02019;s Disease Assessment Scale; MMSE: Mini-Mental State Examination</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></p></sec><sec id="Sec9"><title>Probiotics</title><p id="Par132">Probiotics are living microorganisms that provide health benefits upon ingestion. The most commonly used microorganisms belong to the <italic>Lactobacillus</italic> and <italic>Bifidobacterium</italic> genera. Their beneficial influence on the body is associated with their potential to reduce inflammatory response. Probiotics do not possess LPS and therefore do not induce inflammation [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR120">120</xref>]; moreover, they prevent the leakage of proinflammatory microbial products into the bloodstream by strengthening the integrity of the intestinal epithelium. Additionally, they stimulate the production of brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF), which supports neuronal growth [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR121">121</xref>].</p><p id="Par133">Probiotic supplementation has been shown to increase BDNF levels by modulating gut microbiota composition, enhancing the production of SCFAs, and reducing neuroinflammation and oxidative stress, the factors commonly associated with decreased BDNF levels in patients with AD and PP/PS1 mice [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR122">122</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR123">123</xref>]. Decreased BDNF levels have been implicated in learning and memory deficits observed in AD patients [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR124">124</xref>]. Probiotics have also been implicated in the regulation of neurotransmitters such as serotonin, gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), and dopamine, whose alterations have been linked to AD in animal models and humans [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR19">19</xref>].</p><p id="Par134">Mechanistic evidence in humans supports the beneficial effects of probiotics on cognitive function. Supplementation of middle-aged individuals with <italic>Lactobacillus helveticus</italic>-based fermented drink for 8 weeks improved attention and memory [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR125">125</xref>]. Similarly, supplementation with <italic>Bifidobacterium bifidum</italic> and <italic>Bifidobacterium longum</italic> for 12 weeks in healthy older adults was beneficial for cognition and mental well-being [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR126">126</xref>], implying that probiotic supplementation could improve memory and cognitive function.</p><p id="Par135">Animal studies provide further evidence for the potential of probiotics to modulate AD pathology. Due to the beneficial effects of probiotics on gut microbiota and the nervous system, several studies have explored their potential in modulating AD using animal models of disease (Table <xref rid="Tab5" ref-type="table">5</xref>). In vivo studies using animal models of AD have demonstrated the positive impact of various probiotic strains, including <italic>Lactobacillus plantarum</italic>, <italic>Lactobacillus acidophilus</italic>,<italic> Lactobacillus paracasei</italic>, and <italic>Lactobacillus delbrueckii</italic> subsp. <italic>bulgaricus</italic> and <italic>Lactobacillus brevis</italic>,<italic> Lactobacillus reuteri</italic>,<italic> Lactobacillus rhamnosus</italic>, as well as <italic>Bifidobacterium bifidum</italic>,<italic> Bifidobacterium longum</italic>,<italic> Bifidobacterium breve</italic>,<italic> Bifidobacterium infantis</italic>, and <italic>Streptococcus salivarius</italic> subsp. <italic>thermophilus</italic> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR127">127</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR131">131</xref>]. Overall, those studies indicate that probiotics can prevent memory and learning dysfunction, largely through their effects on A&#x003b2; and Tau pathology. These benefits are accompanied by reduced oxidative stress and inflammation, as well as improvements in brain glucose metabolism &#x02013; notably, increased GLUT levels in the brain [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR127">127</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR131">131</xref>].</p><p id="Par136">Clinical studies also support the relevance of these findings. In parallel with the data obtained in animal models, administration <italic>of Bifidobacterium breve</italic> A1 to AD patients with MCI for 24 weeks improved cognitive performance, as measured by the MMSE test [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR132">132</xref>] (Table <xref rid="Tab6" ref-type="table">6</xref>). Although this was a single-arm trial, its findings are supported by another study in which <italic>Bifidobacterium breve</italic> A1 was administered for 16 weeks to patients with MCI, resulting in improved cognition as assessed by the Battery for the Assessment of Neuropsychological Status, compared to the control group [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR133">133</xref>]. A positive influence on cognitive performance, as measured by the MMSE test, was also observed following a 6-week administration of <italic>Escherichia coli</italic> Nissle 1917. The improvement was associated with reduced oxidative stress, inflammation, and intestinal permeability [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR134">134</xref>].</p><p id="Par137">However, some human studies report inconsistent results. A study with a multispecies probiotic containing <italic>Lactobacillus</italic> and <italic>Bifidobacterium</italic> strain, administered for 12 weeks, reported no measurable impact on memory, inflammation, and oxidative stress markers, suggesting a time-limited or strain-specific effect of the supplementation [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR135">135</xref>]. Although these human trials do not present consistent evidence for the efficacy of probiotics in slowing AD progression, they collectively demonstrate that probiotics can modulate several physiological and metabolic processes implicated in the pathogenesis of AD.</p><p id="Par138">The effectiveness of probiotic supplementation may be enhanced through co-supplementation strategies. AD patients treated for 12 weeks with a probiotic formulation containing <italic>Lactobacillus</italic> and <italic>Bifidobacterium</italic> strains, combined with selenium, exhibited improved cognitive performance. These benefits were accompanied by reductions in oxidative stress, systemic inflammation, and serum lipid levels [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR136">136</xref>]. In vivo studies using mouse models of AD further suggest that probiotics can be co-administered with a wide range of compounds, including lipid-soluble vitamins A and D, B-group vitamins, omega-3 fatty acids, and even physical exercise [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR137">137</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR138">138</xref>]. Such a combination has been shown to enhance cognitive performance and is associated with decreased A&#x003b2; burden and oxidative stress (Table <xref rid="Tab5" ref-type="table">5</xref>).</p><p id="Par139">Several randomized, double-blind, and placebo-controlled clinical trials have investigated the effects of probiotic supplementation in AD patients, yielding mixed results. Therefore, co-supplementation with additional bioactive compounds or dietary strategies that confer complementary benefits may be both valuable and necessary for maximizing therapeutic efficacy. Collectively, the data indicate that while probiotic supplementation modulates several pathways implicated in AD progression, the effect may not be sufficient on its own for all individuals with AD. Although these findings suggest a potential role for probiotics in modulating neuroinflammatory and metabolic processes relevant to AD, the studies conducted to date are limited in number and heterogeneous in design. Consequently, the current evidence remains preliminary, emphasizing the need for larger, multicenter clinical trials with standardized formulations and outcome measures to validate these promising observations.</p><p id="Par140">
<table-wrap id="Tab5"><label>Table 5</label><caption><p>Effects of probiotic interventions on Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (AD) pathology and cognitive functions in preclinical models</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Intervention</th><th align="left">Research model</th><th align="left">Effects</th><th align="left">References</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Probiotic (<italic>Lactobacillus plantarum</italic>)</td><td align="left">Albino (Wistar strain) rats (D-galactose-induced AD model)</td><td align="left">Ameliorated cognitive deficits; reduced A&#x003b2; and tau pathology;</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR127">127</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Probiotic (mixed strains)</td><td align="left">Wistar rats (AD model induced by intracerebroventricular injection of A&#x003b2;)</td><td align="left">Improved memory; reduced oxidative stress</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR128">128</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Probiotic (SLAB51 formulation, lactic acid bacteria + bifidobacteria)</td><td align="left">3xTg-AD mice</td><td align="left">Slowed cognitive decline; reduced A&#x003b2; and tau pathology;</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR129">129</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Probiotic (<italic>Bifidobacterium breve</italic> A1)</td><td align="left">ddY mice (AD model induced by intracerebroventricular injection of A&#x003b2;)</td><td align="left">Prevented cognitive impairment; reduced inflammation and immune gene expression</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR130">130</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Probiotic + vitamins (A, D, B), omega-3, exercise</td><td align="left">APP/PS1 transgenic mice</td><td align="left">Enhanced cognition; reduced A&#x003b2; burden and oxidative stress</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR137">137</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR138">138</xref>]</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Abbreviations: 3xTg-AD: triple transgenic mouse model of AD; A&#x003b2;: Amyloid beta; AD: Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease; APP/PS1: amyloid precursor protein/presenilin 1 double transgenic mouse of AD; ddY: Deutschland&#x02013;Daruma&#x02013;Yoken (outbred mouse strain)</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></p><p id="Par141">
<table-wrap id="Tab6"><label>Table 6</label><caption><p>Effects of probiotic interventions on cognitive functions and metabolic parameters in Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease (AD) patients</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Intervention</th><th align="left">Type of the study</th><th align="left">Effects</th><th align="left">References</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Probiotic + selenium (<italic>Lactobacillus &#x00026; Bifidobacterium</italic> )</td><td align="left">Randomized, double-blind, controlled clinical trial, AD patients</td><td align="left">Improved cognition; reduced oxidative stress, inflammation, and lipid levels</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR136">136</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Probiotic (<italic>Bifidobacterium bifidum &#x00026; Bifidobacterium longum</italic>)</td><td align="left">Randomized, double-blind controlled trial, AD patients</td><td align="left">Improved cognition and mental well-being</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR126">126</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Probiotic (<italic>Lactobacillus helveticus</italic>)</td><td align="left">Randomized, double-blind controlled trial, AD patients</td><td align="left">Improved attention and memory</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR125">125</xref>]</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Multispecies probiotic (<italic>Lactobacillus &#x00026; Bifidobacterium</italic>)</td><td align="left">Randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial, AD patients</td><td align="left">No improvement in memory, inflammation, or oxidative stress</td><td align="left"> [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR135">135</xref>]</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Abbreviation: AD: Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></p></sec><sec id="Sec10"><title>Short-chain fatty acids in AD</title><p id="Par142">Intestinal microbiota produce a wide range of physiologically important metabolites, including SCFAs, bile acids, and neurotransmitters such as GABA, histamine, adrenaline, and acetylcholine [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR139">139</xref>]. Therefore, alterations in gut microbiota composition observed during AD progression are likely associated with changes in metabolite levels [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR140">140</xref>]. Strategies aimed at modulating these metabolites may offer a valuable approach to slowing or preventing AD development.</p><p id="Par143">SCFAs are saturated aliphatic organic acids with up to six carbon atoms, produced by gut microbiota through the fermentation of dietary fibers - carbohydrates that are indigestible by humans. About 95% of SCFAs consist of acetate, butyrate, and propionate, while the remaining 5% include valeric acid, caproic acid, and their branched-chain variants, such as isobutyric and isovaleric acids [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR141">141</xref>]. In a healthy state, SCFAs play an important biological role. They enter the bloodstream and are absorbed by cells via various transport systems, including monocarboxylate transporters (MCTs), which are also present in the brain [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR142">142</xref>]. SCFAs serve as an energy source and regulate glucose, cholesterol, and fatty acids metabolism [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR142">142</xref>]. Additionally, they support the integrity of both the intestinal barrier and BBB through their anti-inflammatory properties [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR143">143</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR144">144</xref>].</p><p id="Par144">Studies have reported changes in the SCFAs levels in the feces [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR140">140</xref>] and brain tissues of AD mouse models [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR71">71</xref>]. Interventions aimed at preventing AD &#x02013; such as FMT, probiotic administration, or antibiotic treatment &#x02013; alter gut microbiota composition and are associated with modulation of SCFA levels [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR85">85</xref>]. However, a study in 31-week-old GF TE4 mice treated orally with SCFAs for five weeks showed increased glial cell activation and elevated phosphorylated tau pathology in the hippocampus [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR89">89</xref>], suggesting that the effects of SCFA supplementation depend on both SCFA composition and concentration and the presence or absence of gut microbiota.</p><p id="Par145">Fecal levels of nearly all types of SCFAs are reduced in cognitively impaired individuals compared to healthy controls [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>]. Moreover, the progression from MCI to AD is associated with a further decrease in formic acid, acetic acid, propanoic acid, 2-methylbutyric acid, and isovaleric acid [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>], suggesting that both the development and progression of AD are linked to declining SCFA levels. The decline of SCFA levels may be the result of a decreased abundance of bacteria in the <italic>Firmicutes</italic> and <italic>Clostridia</italic> clusters observed in AD patients [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>]. Notably, a case of FMT translation in 90-year-old women with AD that resulted in cognitive improvement was accompanied by increased SCFA concentrations [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR91">91</xref>].</p><p id="Par146">SCFAs may influence AD development through several mechanisms. One of the central features of AD pathogenesis is the formation of protein aggregates, and SCFAs have been shown to modulate the process. Valeric acid, butyric acid, and propionic acid exhibit in vitro inhibitory activity against A&#x003b2;1&#x02013;40 and A&#x003b2;1&#x02013;42 aggregation, with valeric and butyric acid also preventing A&#x003b2;1&#x02013;42 fibril formation [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR145">145</xref>]. Moreover, butyrate downregulates BACE1 expression, thereby reducing amyloidogenic processing of APP [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR146">146</xref>]. SCFAs also counteract oxidative stress induced by A&#x003b2; accumulation [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR147">147</xref>]. In vitro studies support these findings. Four weeks of butyrate treatment in 8-week-old 5xFAD mice reduced A&#x003b2; deposition and neuroinflammation, as evidenced by lower TNF-&#x003b1; and IL-6 levels; however, cognitive decline was not observed at that age [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR148">148</xref>]. Extending the treatment to 12 weeks led to reduced A&#x003b2; accumulation and improved hippocampus/amygdala-dependent memory [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR149">149</xref>].</p><p id="Par147">Conversely, supplementation of GF APPPS1 mice with a mixture of three major SCFAs led to increased A&#x003b2; deposition, possibly due to altered microbial activity and impaired A&#x003b2; clearance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR150">150</xref>]. These findings suggest that the impact of SCFA supplementation on AD pathology may depend on the presence and the composition of gut microbiota, as well as the specific type of SCFAs used.</p><p id="Par148">One of the mechanisms through which SCFAs exert their effects is epigenetic modulation, particularly via histone acetylation. Histone deacetylases (HDACs) are enzymes responsible for removing acetyl groups from histones, thereby tightening chromatin structure and reducing gene expression. SCFA, particularly butyrate, acts as an HDAC inhibitor, leading to increased histone acetylation. Altered acetylation patterns have been implicated in neurodegenerative diseases, including AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR151">151</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR152">152</xref>]. In Tg2576 mice, treatment with 4-phenylbutyrate restored histone acetylation and improved spatial memory [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR153">153</xref>]. Similarly, the butyrate treatment improved memory in APPPS1-21 mice, even when administered at advanced stages of the disease [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR152">152</xref>].</p><p id="Par149">The microglial cells appear to be central mediators of SCFAs in AD. Conditional depletion of HDACs specifically in microglia of 5xFAD mice led to reduced A&#x003b2; deposition and improved cognitive performance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR154">154</xref>]. SCFAs also upregulate BDNF, which supports synaptic plasticity [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR146">146</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR155">155</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR156">156</xref>], decrease pro-inflammatory cytokines [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR156">156</xref>], and enhance BBB integrity by increasing the expression of tight-junction proteins [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR144">144</xref>].</p><p id="Par150">Impaired glucose metabolism, thus insufficient energy supply, is a consistent feature associated with the development of AD. This reduced glucose utilization may result from both limited substrate availability and mitochondrial dysfunction. SCFAs may serve as an alternative energy source. For instance, butyrate is a primary energy substrate for colonocytes, covering up to 70% of their energy needs [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR157">157</xref>]. Additionally, studies using <sup>14</sup>C-labeled butyrate demonstrated that it can also be metabolized by the brain [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR158">158</xref>].</p><p id="Par151">Moreover, propionate supplementation in 5xFAD mice - an established AD model &#x02013; was shown to modulate mitochondrial homeostasis by influencing the expression of genes involved in mitophagy and mitochondrial fission. These changes were associated with improved cognitive performance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR159">159</xref>].</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec11"><title>Dietary interventions in AD</title><p id="Par152">Interventions such as antibiotics, probiotics, and fecal microbiota transplantation, although not always consistent, demonstrate that modulation of microbial communities can influence amyloid burden, neuroinflammation, and cognitive performance (Tables <xref rid="Tab1" ref-type="table">1</xref>, <xref rid="Tab2" ref-type="table">2</xref>, <xref rid="Tab3" ref-type="table">3</xref>, <xref rid="Tab4" ref-type="table">4</xref>, <xref rid="Tab5" ref-type="table">5</xref> and <xref rid="Tab6" ref-type="table">6</xref>). In addition to these interventions, another major factor shaping gut microbiota is diet, which also delivers metabolites and nutrients that directly affect brain function [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">75</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR125">125</xref>]. While there are no studies directly linking diet-induced microbial changes with cognitive outcomes in AD patients, it is notable that the impact of ketogenic and Mediterranean diets (MeD) on cognitive outcomes has been vastly investigated [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR165">165</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR167">167</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR171">171</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR176">176</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR199">199</xref>]. The relevance of these dietary approaches stems from their distinct but complementary mechanisms: the ketogenic diet provides ketone bodies as an alternative energy source in cerebral hypometabolism, one of the earliest features of AD development [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR55">55</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR160">160</xref>], whereas the Mediterranean diet supplies anti-inflammatory and antioxidant compounds, including omega-3 fatty acids, which have been associated with a reduced risk of AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>]. Together, these approaches highlight diet as both a modulator of gut microbiota and a potential modulator of AD.</p><sec id="Sec12"><title>Ketogenic diet</title><p id="Par153">The ketogenic diet mimics a fasting state, in which the body shifts from glucose metabolism to alternative energy sources. The intervention typically consists of high intake of fats (providing 60&#x02013;90% of energy), low carbohydrates (5&#x02013;10% of energy), and moderate protein (1&#x02013;1.7 g per kg of body weight) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR161">161</xref>]. The inadequate carbohydrate intake leads to glucose levels that are insufficient to meet the metabolic demands of the brain. To compensate, the body metabolizes fatty acids to acetyl-CoA. Under normal conditions, acetyl-CoA enters the Krebs cycle, but this process requires sufficient oxoacetate derived from pyruvate, an end-product of glycolysis. In ketosis, oxoacetate becomes limited, leading to an excess of acetyl-CoA, which is then diverted to form ketone bodies, that is, acetoacetate, acetone, and beta-hydroxybutyrate [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR162">162</xref>]. These ketones cross the BBB via MCT and serve as an alternative energy source for the brain [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR162">162</xref>]. Importantly, during nutritional ketosis, blood glucose remains within normal range due to gluconeogenesis from glucogenic amino acids and glycerol- released from the breakdown of triacylglycerols.</p><p id="Par154">AD shares metabolic abnormalities with diabetes, particularly impaired ability to effectively utilize glucose [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR55">55</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR160">160</xref>]. This dysfunction is linked to reduced levels of GLUTs and impaired insulin signaling, which contributes to cerebral glucose hypometabolism, one of the earliest features in AD patients [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR50">50</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR51">51</xref>]. Notably, this hypometabolism may be glucose-specific, as the uptake of ketone bodies, such as acetoacetate, remains unaffected. This observation suggests that ketones could serve as an alternative energy source for the aging brain [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR163">163</xref>]. Supporting this, APP transgenic mice fed a ketogenic diet for 43 days showed a significant reduction in A&#x003b2; levels, although this was not accompanied by improvements in cognitive performance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR164">164</xref>]. In contrast, when APP mice were treated with a ketone mixture via subcutaneous injection, not only was A&#x003b2;-42 reduced, but learning and memory were also significantly improved. These effects were associated with enhanced mitochondrial function and reduced oxidative stress, leading to improved neuronal plasticity [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR165">165</xref>]. Additionally, aged dogs fed a diet rich in medium-chain triglycerides (MChTs) show a rapid increase in ketone levels and improved mitochondrial respiration, effects correlated with reduced A&#x003b2; deposition [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR166">166</xref>].</p><p id="Par155">Patients with MCI who followed a low-carbohydrate diet for six weeks demonstrated not only improved fasting insulin and glucose levels but also improved memory performance - effects only slightly correlated with insulin changes [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR167">167</xref>]. In another study, clinically diagnosed AD patients who adhered to a ketogenic diet for 12 weeks experienced improvements in daily functioning and quality of life, despite only minimal but insignificant gains in cognitive performance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR23">23</xref>] .</p><p id="Par156">Adherence to a ketogenic diet can be challenging. Therefore, supplementation with exogenous ketone bodies may offer a practical alternative. A 90-day treatment with a ketogenic compound in patients with mild to moderate AD effectively induced ketosis and led to significant cognitive improvements [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR168">168</xref>]. Similarly, elevated blood ketone levels can be achieved through supplementation with MChTs. Patients with mild to moderate AD who received MChTs for 90 days showed significantly improved cognitive scores [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR169">169</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="Sec13"><title>MeD</title><p id="Par157">MeD is characterized by a high intake of vegetables, legumes, fruits, nuts, cereals, and fish, while consumption of meat, poultry, and high-fat dairy products is typically low; alcohol consumption is moderate [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR170">170</xref>]. Epidemiological studies suggest that adherence to MeD is associated with reduced incidence of AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">21</xref>], a finding supported by multiple studies [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR171">171</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR173">173</xref>]. The beneficial effects have been linked to reduced A&#x003b2; deposition [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR174">174</xref>], decreased brain atrophy [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR175">175</xref>], improved cerebral glucose metabolism [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR173">173</xref>], enhanced cerebral perfusion, and altered (A&#x003b2;) 42/40 ratios in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR176">176</xref>].</p><p id="Par158">The MeD can be further optimized by combining it with other interventions. In adults with self-reported memory complaints or MCI, adherence to a combined MeD and ketogenic diet improved memory and was associated with increased cerebral perfusion and acetoacetate uptake [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR177">177</xref>]. Cognitive performance has also been improved through MeD enriched with extra virgin olive oil, nuts, and fish [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR178">178</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR179">179</xref>], which are omega-3-rich foods. Omega-3 fatty acids, including EPA and DHA, are polyunsaturated fats characterized by the double bond at the third carbon from the terminal methyl group. Higher fish consumption and consequently elevated DHA, but not EPA levels, was associated with reduced AD risk [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR178">178</xref>]. However, EPA in the form of phosphatidylcholine has also shown protective effects, including reduced A&#x003b2; burden, decreased tau-hyperphosphorylation, improved mitochondrial functioning, and reduced oxidative stress [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR180">180</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="Sec14"><title>Polyphenols</title><p id="Par159">The beneficial impact of the MeD on cognition could also be associated with the high intake of polyphenol-rich compounds found in abundance in flowers, seeds, peels, and roots of vegetables, legumes, and fruits. Polyphenols are a chemically diverse group, generally characterized by the presence of an aromatic ring (e.g. benzene) with one or more hydroxyl groups attached [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR181">181</xref>]. Based on the precise chemical structures, polyphenols are divided into ellagitannins, phenolic acids, lignans, stilbenes, and flavonoids, the latter being the most abundant and diverse group of polyphenols found in plant-based foods [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR182">182</xref>]. The general chemical structure of flavonoids consists of two aromatic rings with different side chains, connected by a chain of three carbons with variable oxidation and saturation status, which categorizes the compounds into subclasses. Based on the structure, this vast group is divided into flavonols (e.g., quercetin), flavones (e.g., luteolin, diosmin), flavanones (e.g., hesperetin), flavanols (e.g., catechins), isoflavones (e.g., genistein), and anthocyanidins (e.g., malvidin) [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR181">181</xref>].</p><p id="Par160">Polyphenol compounds have gained significant interest due to their impact on key pathological processes involved in AD development. Flavonoids reduce the production of A&#x003b2; protein through an NF&#x003ba;B-dependent mechanism by transcriptionally inhibiting the expression of the key A&#x003b2;-processing enzyme BACE-1 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR183">183</xref>]. Additionally, mixed fractions of polyphenolic compounds isolated from grape seeds have been shown to prevent the formation of A&#x003b2; protein aggregates in vitro [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR184">184</xref>], as well as tau peptide aggregation [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR185">185</xref>]. More importantly, polyphenol (-)-epi-gallocatechin gallate (EGCG) has demonstrated the ability to disintegrate A&#x003b2;-fibrils into smaller amorphous, non-toxic clusters for mammalian cells [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR186">186</xref>]. Administration of pomegranate juice, rich in polyphenol, for 6.5 months starting at six months of age in APPsw/Tg2576 transgenic mice, resulted in decreased levels of soluble A&#x003b2;42 and reduced A&#x003b2; deposition in the brain. These effects were associated with improved learning abilities compared to controls [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR187">187</xref>].</p><p id="Par161">Despite the significant influence of polyphenols on key aspects of AD development, such as A&#x003b2; formation, they have also been implicated in mitigating oxidative stress and neuroinflammation. Polyphenols, particularly flavonoids, exhibit very potent antioxidant properties due to their ability to directly scavenge ROS. Mechanistically, radicals oxidize hydroxyl groups in polyphenols, giving rise to more stable and less active compounds [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR188">188</xref>]. Additionally, polyphenols such as curcumin act indirectly, reducing the production of ROS by mitochondria, which are the main cellular source of these species, and increasing levels of antioxidant GSH [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR189">189</xref>] and superoxide dismutase activity [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR190">190</xref>]. As a result, polyphenol treatment has been shown to reduce oxidative-stress-induced apoptosis [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR191">191</xref>] and inhibit lipid peroxidation, thereby supporting cell membrane integrity and survival [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR189">189</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR192">192</xref>]. Treatment for 3 months of triple transgenic mouse (3XTg-AD) with nobiletin, a compound derived from citrus peels, reduced levels of soluble A&#x003b2; and ROS in the hippocampal regions, and improved short-term memory and cognition [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR193">193</xref>].</p><p id="Par162">Many polyphenols have been identified as anti-inflammatory compounds. Two intensively studied compounds, curcumin and resveratrol, have been shown to inhibit NF-kappa B signaling, thereby reducing proinflammatory cytokine production induced by exposure to A&#x003b2; [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR194">194</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR195">195</xref>]. Additionally, treatment of PPswe/PS1dE9 double-transgenic AD mice with another flavonoid, fisetin, reduced levels of COX-1 and 12-lipoxygenase (LOX), leading to reduced production of proinflammatory eicosanoids [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR196">196</xref>]. As a consequence, polyphenol treatment reduced astrogliosis and microgliosis, hallmarks of AD-associated changes, and prevented the development of memory and learning deficits [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR196">196</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR197">197</xref>].</p><p id="Par163">The fact that polyphenols impact multiple AD-associated pathologies makes them potentially powerful modulators of cognitive decline during development. Indeed, supplementation of middle-aged people with blueberries for 12 weeks improved their cognitive performance [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR198">198</xref>]. In parallel with this, many studies have reported a positive impact on cognition from polyphenol-rich foods. However, the results are inconsistent, as many clinical studies have found no association between polyphenol intake and cognitive decline [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR199">199</xref>]. The main reasons for these discrepancies might be poor oral bioavailability of polyphenols and their limited ability to cross the BBB [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR199">199</xref>]. Additionally, the biological activity of the polyphenols depends not only on their type but is also influenced by their metabolism in the body, particularly by gut microorganisms. The interaction between gut microbiota and polyphenols is bidirectional, as the composition of gut microbiota shapes the spectrum of polyphenol metabolites, and polyphenols in turn shape the gut microbiota, which can directly impact AD-associated pathology [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR200">200</xref>]. Due to the role of gut microbes in polyphenol metabolism, and thus their biological activity, more studies are needed.</p></sec></sec></sec><sec id="Sec15"><title>Conclusions, limitations, and perspectives</title><p id="Par164">The complex and multifaceted nature of AD, driven by a unique combination of genetic and environmental factors in each individual, suggests that a one-size-fits-all treatment approach is unlikely to be successful. Therefore, effective therapeutic strategies should aim to simultaneously address the diverse and interconnected dysfunctions associated with the disease.</p><p id="Par165">Although variable in composition, gut microbiota imbalances are a constant feature of AD development and may contribute to disease progression through inflammatory and metabolic pathways [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR19">19</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR50">50</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR55">55</xref>]. The reviewed evidence highlights the potential of gastrointestinal intervention as both a preventive and therapeutic strategy. In particular, early dietary intervention such as adherence to a ketogenic or Mediterranean diet, along with supplementation of omega-3 fatty acids, may serve as easy, everyday approaches for individuals at risk of developing AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">21</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR23">23</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR167">167</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR180">180</xref>].</p><p id="Par166">Given the role of gut microbiota as a source of neuroactive metabolites such as SCFAs, regular consumption of fermented foods or probiotic supplements has been suggested as a strategy that could support cognitive function [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR19">19</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR120">120</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR129">129</xref>]. However, current clinical studies in AD patients provide insufficient evidence to confirm clear cognitive benefits [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR132">132</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR135">135</xref>]. More invasive approaches, including FMT and antibiotic treatment, are also being explored as potential strategies for modifying disease progression [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR92">92</xref>]&#x02013; [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR93">93</xref>]. These interventions, however, still require further investigation to determine optimal protocols, timing, and patient selection criteria.</p><p id="Par167">Despite promising findings, the current body of evidence linking gut microbiota modulation with AD remains limited by several factors. Many available studies rely on small clinical trials, pilot interventions, or even case studies, which reduces the statistical power and generalizability of the conclusions. Moreover, the results are often inconsistent across studies, with some reporting cognitive improvements while others show no measurable benefit [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR133">133</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR135">135</xref>]. Differences in study design, treatment duration, microbial strains or species used, and patient heterogeneity further complicate the interpretation of outcomes. For instance, while probiotics are generally safe and accessible, their effects appear strain-specific and time-limited [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR135">135</xref>]. Antibiotic-based strategies, although capable of inducing marked shifts in microbiota, carry risks of adverse systemic effects and lack standardized protocols [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR118">118</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR119">119</xref>]. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), on the other hand, has shown potential to modulate amyloid burden and neuroinflammation in preclinical models [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR90">90</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR91">91</xref>], but its translation into clinical practice remains hampered by the scarcity of large, well-controlled trials and unresolved safety considerations [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR94">94</xref>]. Collectively, these limitations highlight the need for more rigorous, large-scale, and longitudinal studies to clarify the true therapeutic potential of microbiota-targeted interventions in AD.</p><p id="Par168">In conclusion, the gut-brain axis presents a promising and underexplored frontier in AD research. Further studies should focus on refining microbiota-targeted therapies, identifying biomarkers for patient stratification, and integrating dietary, microbial, and metabolic interventions into comprehensive personalized strategies for the prevention and management of AD.</p></sec></body><back><glossary><title>Abbreviations</title><def-list><def-item><term>5xFAD</term><def><p id="Par3">Five familial Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease mutations (transgenic mouse model of AD)</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>ABCA7</term><def><p id="Par4">ATP-binding cassette transporter A7</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>ABCB1</term><def><p id="Par5">ATP-binding cassette sub-family B member 1</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>AD</term><def><p id="Par6">Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>AE</term><def><p id="Par7">Adverse effects</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>AhR</term><def><p id="Par8">Aryl hydrocarbon receptor</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>APOE</term><def><p id="Par9">Apolipoprotein E</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>APP/PS1</term><def><p id="Par10">Amyloid precursor protein/presenilin 1 double transgenic mice</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>APP</term><def><p id="Par11">Amyloid &#x003b2; precursor protein</p></def></def-item><def-item><term>A&#x003b2;</term><def><